       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is about twice the labeled maximum daily dose:\n\n- **Immediate-release:** the maximum is 2,550 mg/day. This is usually given as 850 mg three times daily, or 1,000 mg twice daily (2,000 mg/day) in common practice.\n- **Extended-release:** the maximum is 2,000 mg/day, up to 2,500 mg/day in some labels.\n\nA dose this high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity. It may be a typo, for example 850 mg or 1000 mg instead of 1700 mg, or a mix-up between the per-dose and daily amounts.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the IR maximum).\n- **1000 mg PO twice daily with meals** (2,000 mg/day).\n- **A lower starting dose,** such as 500 mg twice daily with meals, if the patient is starting metformin or is being titrated up.\n\nTell me which dose and frequency you want and I'll enter it right away. If you meant a different drug or formulation, let me know that too."
      }
